Title: "What Is Checked"
Id: "8b5700cc-4d7d-480e-a424-eece3673778e"

Text: "`mlg check` runs a fixed pipeline and reports every problem it finds as an
error (the checker emits no warnings). Before checking, unless `mlg.json` sets
`formatOnCheck` to `false`, the collection is formatted, and any top-level item
missing an `Id:` has one generated. The stages, in order, validate:

**Configuration (`mlg.json`).** The file must be readable, valid JSON, and a
JSON object; every field is required (`name`, `version`, `margin`,
`formatOnCheck`, `outputDir`); `name`/`version` are strings, `margin` is a
positive integer, `formatOnCheck` is a boolean, and `outputDir` is a non-empty
relative path inside the collection. The old key `print_margin` is rejected in
favor of `margin`.

**Collection and `toc` structure.** An explicit target must be a `.mlg` file
that is part of the collection. Every directory's `toc` is checked against its
real children: no duplicate entries, every listed name resolves, and every real
child is listed. `toc` lines must be well formed.

**Parsing.** Three layers each report their own errors: the line/indentation
proto layer (including rejecting single-quoted formulations), the structural
group/section layer (missing, out-of-order, or unexpected sections; unknown
top-level groups; heading, clause, formulation, and text-body shape), and the
formulation expression layer (token errors and construct-specific errors such
as `expected top-level ' is '`).

**Prose code fences.** A ```` ```mlg ```` block inside a `Text:` value or
documentation is parsed as real source and reports a syntax error if it fails;
```` ```mlg-fragment ```` and non-`mlg` fences are skipped.

**Identity and uniqueness.** Every top-level item needs an `Id:` holding a
quoted UUID; Ids must be unique. At most one top-level `Writing:` item is
allowed. Command signatures must be unique across definition kinds, and each
operator/function key may have at most one `Disambiguates`. Spec-infix headings
(`\\:...:/`) are allowed only on `Describes`, refined headings (`::`) only on
`Refines`.

**Documentation.** `Describes`, `Defines`, and `States` must include a `called:`
or `written:` item in `Documented:`; `Refines` must include an `adjective:`
item.

**Command references.** Every command reference must resolve to a defined
signature with a matching argument shape (refined references may fall back to
their base command and refinement pieces), and command `when:`/context
requirements are checked at each use site.

**Target symbols.** Every parameter and target symbol a definition introduces
must be typed: a header parameter needs a `when:` fact; a `Describes` target
symbol must be specified directly or through `extends:`/`extends: ... via ...`;
a `Defines` target symbol must be assigned exactly once and must state its type,
either `... is <type>` or a top-level build `\\<type>@<value>` (a bare
`X := {...}` is rejected). Destructured components (`M ::= (X, *)`) are typed
from `extends:`/`via`, `specifies:`, the parameter's `when:` type, or the
right-hand type of a `::=` binding, and need no separate `when:` entry.

**`when:` clauses.** `when:` may only constrain the definition's own parameters,
and only with `<subject> is <type>` or `<subject> \"op\" <target>`.

**Specifications vs. statements.** An `is` specification or infix specification
introduces a symbol, so it is allowed only in binding positions (`exists:`,
`given:`, `forAll:`, `where:`, `when:`, `suchThat:`); in a statement position it
must use the predicate form `is?` or `\\:...?:/`.

**Symbol scope.** Every referenced symbol must be introduced before use.
`::=` introduces symbols (including destructured components); `:=` does not — its
right-hand side must already be in scope.

**Spec facts and operators.** A spec fact `x \"op\" T` is valid only when `T`'s
type provides that operator. Operators resolve as an in-scope value applied as a
call, then a `Disambiguates` entry, then a provided-symbol capability owned by
the operands' type; otherwise the operator is reported unresolved. Member access
`M.*` must resolve to a member of the owner.

**Capabilities, requirements, and builds.** `Requires:`/`Enables:` capabilities
are validated, including `from:` build-backed capabilities and `from: ... as:`
bindings; a provided spec operator's target must be the described item; a
required definition must reference a `Defines:` entry and establish its stated
fact. Build expressions `\\<type>@<value>` (coercion) and `\\<type>@!<value>`
(coercion + encoding) are checked, and `Enables: relation:` declarations select
that behavior through `represents: \\\\coercion` or `represents: \\\\encoding`.

Type predicates, function-type specifications, and the arguments of `is` type
statements are also checked."
Id: "0f8136db-1ce1-426d-b690-9ca405705e49"
